---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CuriosityGenerator from '../../components/CuriosityGenerator';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import curious from '../../assets/pathways/curious.jpg';
---
<Base title="Learn"><div class="station-page">
<section class="station-hero station-hero-split"><div class="station-hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">LEARN / THE CURIOSITY LAB</p><h1>Science starts with <em>noticing.</em></h1><p class="lead">No grades. No gatekeepers. Just small experiments, surprisingly useful questions, and new ways to see the fungi around you.</p><a class="station-button" href="#play">Try an activity ↓</a></div><Image src={curious} alt="Purple mushroom structures seen close up" widths={[500,850]} sizes="(max-width:760px) 100vw,48vw"/></section>
<section class="station-content" id="play"><div class="station-section-head"><h2>THE CURIOSITY GENERATOR</h2><span>PLAY FIRST / SCIENCE AFTER</span></div><div class="station-learning-grid"><div><h2>What happens when you look a little closer?</h2><p>Try an observation exercise, invent an explanation, then discover the scientific habit hiding inside the game. You can participate alone, with a friend, or on a foray.</p><p class="station-footnote">Nothing to submit. Nothing to score. Share only if you want to.</p></div><CuriosityGenerator client:visible /></div></section>
<section class="station-content" id="evidence-journey"><div class="station-section-head"><h2>ONE MUSHROOM. THREE QUESTIONS.</h2><span>DISCOVER / LEARN / INVESTIGATE</span></div>
<div class="station-learning-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">A REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE</p><h2>What does a new sighting actually prove?</h2><p>Imagine a distinctive fungus is recorded hundreds of kilometers from earlier documented occurrences. Before calling it a recent arrival, ask: Could it have been there unnoticed? How complete were the earlier surveys? What would establish when it arrived?</p><p>These questions matter in a published investigation of <i>Clathrus archeri</i> in Ukraine. Follow the researchers' evidence, make your own predictions, and see where the conclusions stop.</p><a class="station-button" href="/investigate/case-studies/clathrus-archeri/">Try the guided investigation →</a><p class="station-footnote">Based on Prylutskyi &amp; Kapets (2024). This is a learning exercise, not an independent confirmation of a recent introduction.</p></div><div><p class="eyebrow">THREE HABITS TO PRACTICE</p><h3>Notice. Question. Test.</h3><ol><li><strong>Notice:</strong> Separate what was observed from what someone inferred.</li><li><strong>Question:</strong> Look for missing observations, sampling bias, and alternative explanations.</li><li><strong>Test:</strong> Ask what additional records or methods could distinguish the explanations.</li></ol><a class="station-text-link" href="/discover/devils-fingers/">Read the editorial story →</a></div></div></section>
<section class="station-content"><div class="station-section-head"><h2>GO DEEPER</h2><span>FIELD / MICROSCOPE / DNA</span></div><div class="station-link-grid"><a href="/investigate/learn/">Explore six ways to do fungal research <span>→</span></a><a href="/investigate/resources/">Find scientific references and databases <span>→</span></a><a href="/investigate/tools/">Understand experimental research tools <span>→</span></a></div></section>
</div></Base>